The compiler owns cast evidence. A nominal type can add source evidence for
one of the supported target families by defining the required method and
declaring explicit conformance:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    Label = |
        text String,
    |

    to_string |this Label| -> String:
        return this.text
    ;

    Label must CASTABLE_TO_STRING
    value String = cast Label("docs")
]

Fifteen source-authored target families have infallible and fallible evidence:
the eleven fixed numeric types plus `Bool`, `String`, `Char` and `Error`.
Fallible evidence returns the target with builtin `Error!`; conversion uses
ordinary failure handling and the same plain `cast` syntax. For one target,
infallible and fallible evidence cannot coexist.

Builtin conversion evidence also covers `Byte` and `Dec`, but Moth defines no
source-authored traits for them. See @./#cast-evidence (Cast evidence) for the
complete trait signatures and conversion rules.
